# RenoEstimate: project rules

## Deployment target
Cloudflare Workers via the OpenNext adapter (`@opennextjs/cloudflare`), configured in `wrangler.jsonc` and `open-next.config.ts`.
(`@cloudflare/next-on-pages` was dropped: it is deprecated, supports Next only up to 15.5.2, and targets Pages, not Workers.)

- `npm run build` runs the full OpenNext build (`opennextjs-cloudflare build`), which calls `npm run build:next` (the plain `next build`) via `buildCommand` in `open-next.config.ts`. Never make `build:next` call OpenNext (infinite recursion).
- Cloudflare dashboard (Workers Builds) works with its defaults: build command `npm run build`, deploy command `npx wrangler deploy` (wrangler detects OpenNext and runs `opennextjs-cloudflare deploy`, which needs the `.open-next` output from the build). `npm run deploy` / `npm run preview` do the same locally.
- The Worker needs the `nodejs_compat` flag (set in wrangler.jsonc).
- `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` variables are inlined at build time: define them as *build* variables. Secrets (`AUTH_SECRET`, `PADDLE_API_KEY`, `PADDLE_WEBHOOK_SECRET`) are runtime secrets (`wrangler secret put` or dashboard).
- Local Worker secrets go in `.dev.vars` (gitignored).

## Runtime rules
- Do NOT add `export const runtime = "edge"` to routes or pages: OpenNext does not support it (everything runs on the Workers runtime, Node-compatible). Middleware is the only edge-style entry and is supported.
- Prefer Web APIs (`fetch`, `crypto.subtle`, `TextEncoder`) over Node built-ins; no `fs`/`path` for request-time work (the Worker has no filesystem).
- `lib/costEstimator.ts`, `lib/design.ts` and `lib/units.ts` are pure and must stay free of I/O and Node APIs.
- Secrets are read from `process.env` inside request handlers only, never at module scope, and never in client components (`lib/paddleServer.ts` imports `server-only`).

## Code style
- Strict TypeScript (TypeScript 5), no `any`. Modules stay small, typed and self-documenting.
